R ewninedwfiapbolewer,nehrygdy telcehctnroilciotgyi,esa, subcih masa s,olhar pomwaedre, mankind. The increased utilization of renewable energy resources will require a drastically increased ability to store charge. As such, the development of efficient energy storage devices has become increasingly crucial.1 The electrochemical capacitors, also called supercapacitors, can store energy in two closely spaced layers with opposing charges and are used to power up electrical vehicles, consumer electronics, memory backups, and military devices.2 Electrochemical supercapacitors bridge the gap between batteries that offer a high energy storage capacity (energy density, ED) but are slow in charge− discharge and conventional capacitors that are comparatively faster for charge−discharge (power density, PD) but have a lower energy storage capacity.
Traditional supercapacitors known as electrochemical double-layer capacitors (EDLCs) typically employ carbonbased materials with high surface area and porosity. EDLCs store energy by forming an ionic double layer which diffuses very quickly, hence providing a very rapid discharge as well as higher power densities. However, the ED of EDLCs and the
Scheme 1. Schematic Illustration Showing the Fabrication of Porous Metal Oxide Nanostructures with Large Surface Area and High Porosity from Metal−Organic Frameworks (MOFs) Composed of Metal Ions and Organic Linkers as Precursors through a Two-Step Annealing in Nitrogen $(\mathbf{N}_{2})$ and Air, Respectively
charges which can be physically stored by EDLCs are quite low in aqueous as well as organic electrolytes.4 Two-dimensional graphene has been increasingly used to provide higher charge− discharge rates; however, it exhibits a lower volumetric capacitance compared to that of traditional porous carbons.5 In general, the most critical factors that determine the capacitive performance of EDLCs are conductivity, surface area, pore volume, pore size distribution, and size of the electrolyte ions in solvated and desolvated shells.6
On the other hand, electrochemically active materials such as transition metal oxides $(\mathrm{TMOs})^{7-10}$ and sulfides11 employ a reversible Faradaic reaction to store charges. TMOs have shown higher capacitance capabilities as well higher energy densities by at least an order of magnitude than those of carbon-based materials. However, the microstructure of the metal oxide materials may become distorted, and its original phase may be changed as a result of these continuous reactions, which ultimately results in poor cycling stability for commercial applications.12 Thus, the development of highly porous metal oxide nanostructures with excellent electrochemical properties is crucial for achieving high ED and PD in EES. The capacitance of TMO materials is strongly governed by several parameters including porosity (to enable a fast access for the electrolyte), crystallinity (to ensure the deep diffusion of the electrolyte), the size of the redox active species, as well as the surface area of the electrode materials (to enhance the contact area between the electrode materials and the electrolyte).13 In order to achieve high ED and PD, it is highly desirable that the electrode material should be used in combination with a suitable electrolyte. However, to date, there is no known perfect electrolyte suitable for all types of electrodes for achieving highperformance next-generation supercapacitors.14 Therefore, there is a strong need to develop an easily scalable, costeffective method for synthesizing advanced functional materials which exhibit high stability, high electrical conductivity, and permanent porosity with tunable pore size and pore volume for high-performance next-generation energy storage applications. As of now, metal oxide nanostructures have been synthesized by a variety of chemical and physical methods. Some of these methods have shown promise as cost-effective methods for the synthesis of metal oxides; however, very few of them can be applied at an industrial-scale level. Hence, as of today, the majority of commercial supercapacitors still heavily rely on carbon-based supercapacitors which have a cycle life of up to 500 000 cycles.1
Metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) can be made by attaching inorganic metal ions to organic linkers via strong chemical bonds, as discovered by Yaghi and Li in the late
1990s.15 Due to the variety of existing metal ions and organic linkers, limitless combinations of MOFs can be developed to suit the targeted application. Depending on the organic and inorganic units, structurally, MOFs can be prepared in onedimension (1-D), two-dimension (2-D), or three-dimension (3-D). This has led to the development of well over 20 000 MOFs in the past two decades.16 Furthermore, the pore size of MOFs can be tuned in order to improve their functional performance. Thus, depending on the need of a specific application, MOF materials can be designed with specific structures, textural characteristics, and dimensions to meet the demands of the applications. Because of these distinctive properties, MOFs have found applications in gas adsorption and desorption,17 drug delivery,18 optoelectronics,19 and catalysis.20
More interestingly, other than their direct use, MOFs can also be used as sacrificial templates to derive various porous nanomaterials by applying different thermal and/or chemical treatments. For instance, the heat treatment of MOFs in an inert atmosphere can produce highly porous carbons with high specific surface areas $({\sim}3000~\mathrm{\m}^{2}~\mathrm{\g}^{-1})$ , given that chemical etching is conducted to remove the surface metal ions. Compared with carbonaceous materials fabricated using conventional precursors, MOF-derived carbons often exhibit controllable porous architectures, pore volumes, and surface areas. Typically, the carbonization of MOFs to produce porous carbons can be performed directly without (“direct carbonization”) or with secondary precursors (e.g., ethylenediamine,21 furfuryl alcohol,22 etc.) (“indirect carbonization”).23 The former is more advantageous as it is more facile and involves only a single calcination step. To date, various porous carbon materials with controlled morphologies from 0-D to 3-D have been successfully derived from the carbonization of MOFs, highlighting the versatility of MOFs as precursors.24−26 Furthermore, MOF-derived carbons can be doped with elements, such as nitrogen (N), phosphorus (P), and/or sulfur (S) to further enhance their functional performance.27−29
On the other hand, the direct heat treatment of MOFs in air can lead to their decomposition into their corresponding metal oxides (Scheme 1).8 Typically, the derived metal oxide nanostructures possess the same structures as those of the parent MOFs (with optimized heating conditions in nitrogen $\bar{{\bf\Phi}}({\bf N}_{2})$ and/or air atmosphere), as well as higher surface areas compared to metal oxides produced by other methods, often with readily developed porous architectures. Moreover, the variations in annealing temperature and time can provide useful control over the composition, surface area, and pore size distribution of MOF-derived oxides.13,23Apart from pure metal oxide nanostructures, porous metal oxide nanocomposites can also be fabricated through the direct heat treatments of bimetallic MOFs or core−shell MOF composites at high temperatures in air or through a two-step annealing, first in $\mathrm{N}_{2},$ then in air (Scheme 2). The latter method is particularly beneficial for preserving the original structure of the parent MOFs and, therefore, for achieving higher surface area and porosity. In addition, MOF-derived oxides can be further hybridized with various carbon-based materials, such as reduced graphene oxide (rGO), carbon nanotubes (CNTs), and 3-D graphene networks as conductive matrices to enhance their electrochemical performance for supercapacitor applications.30 The highly tunable properties of MOF-derived metal oxides along with the possibilities to combine them with highly conductive carbon supports make them attractive candidates for next-generation supercapacitors. In relation to this, we will discuss the recent developments in the fabrication of MOFderived nanoporous metal oxides and metal oxide nanocomposites for supercapacitor applications.
Various MOF Precursors for the Synthesis of Porous Metal Oxides. The electrochemical performance of TMO materials in supercapacitors is strongly governed by the combination of various parameters including surface area, crystallinity, porosity, surface energy, chemical reactivity, and so on. In order to exploit the full potential of transition metal oxides (TMOs) in supercapacitors, it is important to develop porous TMO nanostructures with controlled nanoporous architectures and textural characteristics for achieving the desired electrochemical performance. So far, the synthesis of porous TMO materials is mainly accomplished using wet chemical routes; however, challenges still remain with regard to tailoring the morphology in an easily scalable way with a high degree of reproducibility.46,72,73
MOFs have emerged as attractive templates for the derivations of porous TMO materials due to their tunable composition, highly designable structures, large surface area, and controllable porosity.23 As MOFs are made up of metal ion centers coordinated to organic linkers, their controlled heating in air can give rise to porous TMO nanomaterials due to the oxidation of the metal ions and the release of gaseous $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ originating from the decomposition of organic linkers during the heat treatment. The resulting nanoporous oxide materials typically retain the shape and porosity of the parent MOFs, which are favorable for obtaining large surface area. To date, various porous TMO nanomaterials have been derived from MOF precursors, including cupric oxide $\mathrm{(CuO)},^{31,74}$ cobalt oxide (Co3O4),8,32−38,61,75 iron oxide (Fe2O3),41−43 magnesium oxide (MgO),44 manganese oxide (Mn2O3),45−47 nickel oxide (NiO),48−50 titanium dioxide (TiO2),51−54 zinc oxide (ZnO),55−57,76 and cerium oxide $\left(\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\right),^{40}$ and recently, this has been expanded to mixed TMOs, such as ferrites $\left(\mathrm{CoFe_{2}O_{4}}^{58}\right.$ and $\mathbf{\dot{M}n_{1.8}F e_{1.2}O_{4}}^{60})$ and cobaltites $\left(\mathrm{CuCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4},^{59}\right.$ $\mathrm{ZnCo_{2}O_{4}},^{77}\mathrm{Zn}_{x}\mathrm{Co_{3-x}O_{4}},^{62}$ and $\mathrm{ZnMn}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}^{63}$ ).
More interestingly, MOFs are not limited for the development of single metal oxides but can be useful for the preparation of various mixed metal oxides and metal oxide nanocomposites, such as $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}},^{64}$ ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}/$ $\mathrm{ZnFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4},{}^{65}$ $\mathrm{CuO}/\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ 66 $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{NiO},^{67}$ $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2},^{68}$ $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2},^{69}$ $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4},^{73}$ $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2},^{70}$ 5 $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/$ $\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}^{71}$ $\mathrm{NiFe_{2}O_{4}/F e_{2}O_{3}}^{\mathrm{78}}\mathrm{\NiO}@\mathrm{ZnO},^{\mathrm{79}}\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4},^{\mathrm{80}}$ $\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4},^{81}$ and $\mathrm{ZnO/ZnFe_{2}O_{4}}.$ 82−84 Therefore, by involving multiple functionalities and high surface area, these materials can be designed for achieving high-performance electrodes for supercapacitors. The different precursors used to derive porous metal oxides and the corresponding surface areas are shown in Figure 1. These porous MOF-derived TMO nanomaterials have been employed for a wide variety of applications including lithium-ion batteries (LIBs), supercapacitors, gas sensors, and catalysis.13 However, for this review, we will focus solely on the development of MOFderived metal oxides for supercapacitor applications. In the next few sections, we will discuss the developments of each of these MOF-derived materials including their electrochemical properties and the various parameters which affect their supercapacitor applications.
When the porous $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ polyhedra were employed as a supercapacitor electrode, their capacitive performance was significantly better than those of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanostructures prepared by other methods. The maximum specific capacitance value obtained was $504\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ at a scan rate of $\bar{5}\ \mathrm{m}\bar{\mathrm{V}}\ s^{-1}$ . In order to enhance the energy density, it is important to extend the potential window, as the energy density is directly proportional to the potential. By adding the contributions from two materials, the assembled asymmetric supercapacitor (ASC) was able to exhibit an enhanced energy density in an aqueous electrolyte. Furthermore, when an ASC was assembled using the ZIF-67-derived nanoporous $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ as the positive electrode and ZIF-67-derived nanoporous carbon as the negative electrode, the potential window of the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ material could be extended up to $1.6\mathrm{V}.$ In addition, the assembled ASC showed a high maximum energy density of $36\mathrm{W}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ and a high specific power of 1600 W $\mathrm{i}{\tt g}^{-1}$ at a current density of 2 A $\mathbf{g}^{-\mathrm{{1}}}$ , which is much higher than that of the symmetric supercapacitor based on pure nanoporous carbon or nanoporous ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ . More interestingly, this energy density value is also higher compared to previously reported ASCs based on ${\mathrm{Co}}({\mathrm{OH}})_{2}$ nanorods,90 nanoporous $\mathrm{\DeltaNi(OH)}_{2}\mathrm{,}^{\mathrm{\Delta91}}$ mesoporous $\mathrm{MnO}_{2},\mathrm{\Lambda}^{92}\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$ nanorods,93 and $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ nanoplates.94 A similar “two-for-one” concept has also recently been demonstrated using a 2-D Co-MOF as a single precursor to prepare both electrodes (a $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ cathode and an N-doped carbon nanosheet anode (NC)) for a flexible ASC.95 To obtain the porous 2-D $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanosheets, the Co-MOF particles grown on the carbon cloth (CC) (from the reaction between aqueous solutions of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and 2-methylimidazole) were first calcined in ${\bf N}_{2}$ at $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ at a slow heating rate of $1\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}$ , followed by a secondary calcination in air at $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ at a heating rate of $5^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $\mathrm{min^{-1}}$ . In comparison, the N-doped carbon nanosheets were obtained from the direct calcination of the 2-D Co-MOF particles in $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ at $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ under a slow heating rate of $1~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $\mathbf{\bar{m}}\mathbf{\dot{n}}^{-1}$ , followed by an immersion in a $^{\mathrm{~1~M~}}$ ferric chloride solution. The assembled $\mathrm{CC}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}//\mathrm{CC}@\mathrm{NC}$ flexible ASC could deliver a maximum energy density of $41.5\mathrm{W}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ at a power density of $6.2\ensuremath{\mathrm{\kW\kg^{-1}}}$ , and it could maintain a high energy density of $27.4\mathrm{Wh}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ at a peak power density of 49.2 kW $\bar{\mathbf{k}}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . Furthermore, when the $\mathrm{CC}@\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}//\mathrm{CC}@\mathrm{NC}$ full cell was cycled under various bending/twisting conditions, it could retain $90.8\%$ and $85.5\%$ of the initial specific capacitance after 10 000 and 20 000 cycles, respectively, indicating its good structural stability and excellent cycling stability.
Apart from $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4},$ manganese oxides $(\mathrm{Mn}_{x}\mathrm{O}_{y})$ have also been widely used in supercapacitor applications due to their high theoretical capacitance of ${\sim}600\ \mathrm{~F~}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ as an electrochemical pseudocapacitor.46 Porous manganese oxide nanostructures are most commonly derived from Mn-BTC (nanobars, nanospheres; BTC, benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylic acid),45,46 which can be synthesized through the solvothermal reaction between manganese(II) nitrate and trimesic acid $\mathrm{(H_{3}B T C)}$ at mild temperatures $(\sim120-160~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , as well as Mn-MIL-100.47 Previously, well-crystallized mesoporous $\mathrm{{Mn}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}_{3}$ nanobars with a moderate specific surface area of $44~\mathrm{~m}^{2}~\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ have been obtained from the direct pyrolysis of Mn-BTC nanobars at 650 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $3\mathrm{~h~}$ .46 The cyclic voltammetry (CV) studies of these $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ nanobars showed an increasing trend in their current response with increasing scan rates. The maximum capacitance value obtained using the standard three electrode system was $250\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ at a current density of $0.2\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ . Furthermore, the ASC assembled using the commercial activated carbon (AC) as a counter electrode and the synthesized $\mathrm{{Mn}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}_{3}$ nanobars as the positive electrode could deliver a high energy density of $147\mathrm{W}$ $\mathrm{\hat{h}\ k g^{-1}}$ and a power density of $100\bar{4}~\mathrm{W~kg^{-1}}$ .
Hierarchical cerium oxide $\left(\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\right)$ with dumbbell-like structures has previously been derived from the MOF CeBTC at room temperature.40 The MOF precursor Ce-BTC was directly precipitated at room temperature following the addition of cerium nitrate $(\mathrm{Ce}(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\bar{)_{2}})$ into a water/ethanol mixture $\left(\mathbf{v}/\mathbf{v}=3{:}1\right)$ of $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ . To achieve the full conversion to ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2},$ the Ce-BTC dumbbell-like particles were treated with $^{2\mathrm{~M~}}$ potassium hydroxide (KOH) for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ at room temperature, with no further heat treatment. Structurally, each individual dumbbell is composed of many nanorods, which themselves are constructed of many small nanoparticles, thereby giving rise to a large specific surface area of $2\boldsymbol{\hat{0}}5\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , with a narrow pore size distribution centered at $5\ \mathrm{~nm}$ . The electrochemical properties of the dumbbell-like $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ particles were investigated in three different electrolytes, including 2 M KOH, 2 M $\mathrm{KOH}~+~0.05$ M ${\mathrm{K}}_{4}{\mathrm{Fe}}({\mathrm{CN}})_{6},$ and $2\ \mathrm{\:\:M\:\:\:KOH}\ +\ 0.1\ \mathrm{\:\:M}$ $\mathrm{K_{4}F e(C N)_{6}}.$ . The CV curves of ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ at different scan rates from 5 to $100\mathrm{~mV~}\mathrm{{s}^{-1}}$ in all three electrolytes revealed the potential window of this material to be 0.0−0.35 V. Furthermore, a well-defined pair of redox peaks for all of the sweep rates was observed, which was attributed to the Faradaic redox reaction of $\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}/\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}$ . In this system, the possible redox reactions of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ involved two simultaneous processes: (i) surface adsorption−desorption of $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ and (ii) intercalation− deintercalation of $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ into or from the $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ surface. These processes can be represented by the following equations:
Non‐Faradaic:
$$
\left(\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\right)\mathrm{surface}+\mathrm{K}^{+}+e^{-}\rightleftharpoons\left(\mathrm{CeO}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{K}^{+}\right)\mathrm{surface}
$$
Faradaic: $\mathrm{Ce}^{\mathrm{IV}}\mathrm{O}_{2}+\mathrm{K}^{+}+e^{-}\leftrightarrows\mathrm{Ce}^{\mathrm{III}}\mathrm{O}{\cdot}\mathrm{OK}$
The addition of a $\mathrm{K}_{4}\mathrm{Fe(CN)}_{6}$ redox-active additive into the KOH electrolyte was found to increase the specific capacitance of the $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ electrode, with the specific capacitance in $^{2\mathrm{~M~}}$ $\mathrm{KOH}+0.05\mathrm{~M~}\mathrm{K}_{4}\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}$ electrolyte being $434\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ at a current density of 1 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . In comparison, the specific capacitance in 2 M KOH solution was up to $235\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ at a current density of 1 A $\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ . Interestingly, when the concentration of $\mathrm{K_{4}F e(C N)_{6}}$ was increased from 0.05 to 0.1 M, a higher specific capacitance of $779\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ was obtained at 1 A $\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ . The enhancement in specific capacitance was promoted by the lower intrinsic charge transfer resistance as well as the lower diffusion resistance, as determined by the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements. Moreover, the addition of ${\bar{\mathrm{K}}}_{4}{\mathrm{Fe}}({\mathrm{CN}})_{6}$ was also beneficial for improving the cycling stability of the Ce-BTC-derived $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ electrode with $100\%$ capacity retention achieved in 2 M $\mathrm{:\KOH~+~0.1~\M}$ $\mathrm{K_{4}F e(C N)_{6}}$ electrolyte after 10 000 cycles at $10\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}.$ , as opposed to only $91\%$ in $^{2\mathrm{~M~}}$ KOH electrolyte.
As a promising electrode material, nickel oxide (NiO) has been extensively investigated for supercapacitor applications due to its outstanding theoretical specific capacitance $2583\mathrm{~F~}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ , high abundance, low cost, as well as high thermal and chemical stabilities. Previously, porous NiO polyhedra with sizes ranging from 0.5 to $1\ \mu\mathrm{m}$ have been obtained from the direct heat treatment of Ni-MOF $(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HCOO})_{6})$ polyhedra in air at $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .50 The octahedral-shaped Ni-MOF precursor was produced by incubating a mixture of hydrated nickel(II) nitrate and formic acid in $N,N$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\ \mathrm{~h~}.$ . During the heat treatment in air, bound water molecules were removed at $110{-}180\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ whereas the removal of bound DMF and the decomposition of Ni-MOF to NiO occurred between 260 and $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The resulting NiO polyhedra were assembled from 2-D nanosheets made up of small, uniform nanoparticles with large quantities of pores between the nanoparticles, thus leading to a moderate surface area of $34~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The CV curves of the NiO polyhedra at different scan rates $\left(10{-}200~\mathrm{mV}~s^{-1}\right)$ in the range of $0.1\mathrm{-}0.55$ V revealed the presence of distinct redox peaks, indicating that the pseudocapacitance of the synthesized NiO material originated from the surface oxidation/reduction reactions of $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}$ . The specific capacitances of the NiO polyhedra were measured to be 322, 311, 244, 204, and $149\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ at current densities of 1, 2, 10, 20, and $40\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , respectively, corresponding to a capacity retention of $46\%$ at the highest current density of $40\bar{\mathrm{~A~g~}}^{-1}$ . Furthermore, the MOF-derived NiO electrode was highly stable with a very small loss in specific capacitance after 400 consecutive cycles.
Beside single-component TMOs, MOFs have also been used to derive porous mixed metal oxide nanostructures as multiple metallic species can exist within the same framework.23 Among mixed metal oxides, spinel zinc cobaltite $(\mathrm{ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4})$ has been considered as a promising alternative to $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ for supercapacitor applications because of its higher conductivity and richer redox reactions, promoted by the coupling of two metal species. For instance, Chen et al. have previously reported the fabrication of porous $\mathrm{ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles from a mixed $Z\mathrm{n}\cdot$ - Co-MOF, $\mathrm{Zn}\bar{\mathrm{Co}}_{2}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}(\mathrm{DMF}){\cdot}\bar{\mathrm{H}_{2}}\mathrm{O}$ (JUC-155, $\mathrm{JUC}=\mathrm{Jilin}$ University China).77 This particular MOF was synthesized by adding a mixture of zinc chloride, cobalt(II) chloride, BTC, and DMF into a secondary solution containing DMF and triethylamine, followed by a subsequent heating at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . Structurally, JUC-155 contains one zinc ion, two cobalt ions, six crystallographically equivalent BTC ligands, one central bridging oxygen atom, and one terminal DMF molecule. The conversion of JUC-155 to $\mathrm{ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ was achieved through a direct calcination in air at $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ The specific surface area of the obtained porous $\mathrm{ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles was considerably decreased from 55.0 to $20.4~\mathrm{~m~}^{2}~\mathrm{~g~}^{-1}$ with the increase in calcination temperature from 400 to $600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ due to the increase in crystal size at higher temperatures.
When employed as a supercapacitor electrode, the porous $\mathrm{ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles obtained at calcination temperatures of 400, 500, and $600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ exhibited specific capacitances of 457, 335, and $244\mathrm{F}\ g^{-1}.$ , respectively, at a current density of $1\mathrm{Ag^{-1}}$ . When the current density was increased to 20 A $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{g}^{-1}}\end{array}$ , the specific capacitances decreased to 368, 240, and $166\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}},$ respectively. The discharge capacitances of the three electrodes decreased with the increase in charge−discharge current densities due to the resistance increase in $\mathrm{ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{\dot{O}}_{4}$ and the relatively insufficient Faradaic redox reaction at higher current densities. Furthermore, when tested at a current density of 2 A $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{g}^{-1}.}\end{array}$ , only a small drop in specific capacitance was noted after 1500 cycles, with the specific capacitances remained at approximately 425 $(97.9\%)$ , 313 $(96.8\%)$ , and $212\ensuremath{\mathrm{~F~}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{g}}^{-1}$ $(\bar{94}.6\%)$ for porous $\mathrm{ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles obtained at calcination temperatures of 400, 500, and $600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ respectively. The superior electrochemical performance of MOF-derived mixed oxides compared to the pure oxide ones for supercapacitor applications has also been observed in a recent report by Jayakumar et $a l.^{96}$ In this work, $\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{3-x}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ hollow nanocages were prepared from ZIF-67 particles containing different $\mathrm{{Ni}/\mathrm{{Co}}}$ ratios $\mathrm{\left(Ni/Co=1:1\right.}$ , 0.5:1, and 1:5:1), and the $\mathrm{{Ni}/\mathrm{{Co}}}$ ratio had a profound effect on the composition of the final products. The mixed oxide $\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{Co}=0.5{:}1$ sample exhibited peaks closer to pure spinel $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ due to the low amount of nickel, whereas the mixed oxide sample prepared using $\mathrm{{Ni}/\mathrm{{Co}=1:1}}$ showed peaks which closely resembled the peaks of spinel ${\mathrm{NiCo}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{4},$ where nickel lodges itself in the octahedral sites and cobalt occupies both tetrahedral and octahedral sites. In contrast, the $\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{3-x}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ product made using $\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{Co}~=~1.5{:}1$ showed a higher formation of NiO compared to that obtained using $\mathrm{{Ni}/\mathrm{{Co}=1:1}}$ . These results suggest that the addition of Ni into the system promoted the replacement of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ from the spinel structure of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4},$ and when the Ni content is high enough, NiO is formed on the outer layer of the sample. The electrochemical measurements of these $\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{3-x}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ samples revealed that the optimized $\mathrm{Ni/}$ $C o=1{:}1$ sample showed an extremely high specific capacitance of $1931\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}.$ compared to 1626 and $493.3\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{Co}=1.5{:}1$ and $\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{Co}=0.5{:}1$ samples, respectively. Furthermore, the optimized $\mathrm{{Ni}/\mathrm{{Co}=1:1}}$ sample exhibited a good capacitance retention of $69.5\%$ after 5000 cycles at a current density of 1 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . The poorer electrochemical properties of the $\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{3-x}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ samples prepared using $\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{Co}~=~0.5{:}1$ and $\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{Co}~=~1.5{:}1$ compared to that obtained using $\mathrm{{Ni}/\mathrm{{Co}=1:1}}$ were attributed to the lack of redox centers and the presence of thick NiO layers, respectively, which reduced the diffusion efficiency of the electrolyte.
In a related report, hollow $\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanowall arrays have been successfully fabricated on a flexible carbon cloth substrate by using uniform 2-D Co-MOF solid nanowall arrays as the starting precursors.97 In the proposed method, 2-D Co-MOF nanowall arrays were first deposited on a carbon cloth substrate by reacting aqueous solutions of cobalt(II) nitrate and 2- methylimidazole. In the second step, the MOF nanowall arrays were dispersed in an ethanolic solution of nickel(II) nitrate, which promoted a hydrolysis-controlled ion exchange and etching process according to the equation:
$$
\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}+2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\leftrightarrows\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}+2\mathrm{H}^{+}
$$
in which the $\mathbf{H}^{+}$ ions would etch the Co-MOF and promote the deposition of $\mathrm{{Ni-Co}}$ layered double hydroxide $\left(\mathrm{Ni-Co\LDH}\right)$ on the surface of the nanowalls, thus resulting in a hollow wall structure. Finally, the hollow and porous $\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanowall arrays could be obtained from the thermal decomposition of the Ni−Co LDH in air at $350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . When tested as a supercapacitor electrode using a three-electrode system in 2.0 M KOH electrolyte, the $\mathrm{CC}@\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanowall arrays exhibited a high specific capacitance of $\dot{1}055.3\mathrm{~F~}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $\bar{2.5}\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ , which could be maintained at $483.3\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ even when the current density was increased by 24 times to $60\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , corresponding to a capacitance retention of $45.8\%$ . In comparison, the $\mathbf{CC}@\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ electrode displayed a lower specific capacitance of $823.4\mathrm{F}\ g^{-1}$ at $\mathsf{S\ m A\ c m^{-2}}$ , which is further decreased by $44.3\%$ when the current density was raised by 16 times. The higher specific capacitance and improved rate performance of the $\mathrm{CC}@\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ electrode resulted from the hollow and porous structures of the $\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanowall arrays which could provide numerous reaction sites and shorten the ion diffusion length. The flexible ASC assembled using the fabricated $\mathrm{CC}@\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ as the cathode and nitrogen-doped carbon flakes on carbon cloth $(\mathrm{CC}@\mathrm{NC})$ as the anode (with a poly(vinyl alcohol) (PVA)-KOH solid-state electrolyte) could deliver a maximum energy density of $31.9\mathrm{W}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ at a power density of $2.9\mathrm{~kW~kg^{-1}}.$ , and the value only slightly decreased to 27.3 W h $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ at a peak power density of $2\bar{2}.9\ \mathrm{kW\kg^{-1}}$ .
Despite the existence of many reports on the fabrication of MOF-derived metal oxides, efforts for the development of supercapacitors using these materials are very limited. This may be due to several factors, including the following:
(1) The lack of pre-nitrogen treatment, which leads to the destruction of the inherent morphology of the parent MOF, leads to a drastic decrease in both surface area and porosity.   
(2) At lower annealing temperatures, undesired carbon bonding introduces high resistance in the sample.
In order to make highly efficient supercapacitors out of MOFderived TMO nanomaterials, it is crucial that these issues are addressed immediately.
MOF-Derived Binary Metal Oxide Composites. Apart from pure TMOs, MOFs have also been utilized as precursors or templates for fabricating porous TMO nanocomposites consisting of two metal oxide constituents (i.e., binary TMO nanocomposites). Binary TMO nanocomposites have been shown to be superior to pure metal oxide nanostructures for supercapacitor applications as (i) they can provide multiple redox reactions and (ii) they exhibit higher electrical conductivity, owing to the relatively low activation energy or electron transfer between the metal cations.23,98 Typically, porous metal oxide nanocomposites can be synthesized from MOFs through two main methods: (i) by coating a secondary layer of MOF on the surface of the MOF core to form core− shell MOF composites and (ii) by creating bimetallic MOFs composed of two different metal ions.
Supercapacitor electrode materials based on hollow starfishshaped $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/Z n F e_{2}O_{4}}$ nanocomposites have previously been fabricated from partially $\mathrm{Fe(III)}$ -substituted MOF-5 $\mathrm{\Co_{3}O_{4}}\mathrm{-}$ ${\mathrm{Fe}}^{{\mathrm{III}}}{-}{\mathrm{MOF}}{-}{\bar{S}},$ ).65 To synthesize these composites, Fe, Zn precursors, and terephthalic acid $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC)}$ were initially added into a DMF−ethanol dispersion of ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ nanocages, before being reacted at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Following calcination in air at ${500}^{\mathrm{~\circ}}\mathrm{C},$ $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/Z n F e_{2}O_{4}}$ hollow nanocomposites were obtained from the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}–\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -MOF-5 template. In this starfish-shaped structure, the ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ nanocages formed the “starfish head” and the porous nanotubes assembled of small ${\mathrm{ZnFe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ nanoparticles from ${\mathrm{Fe}}^{{\mathrm{III}}}{-}{\mathrm{MOF}}{-}{5}$ formed the “starfish arms”. In terms of formation mechanism, the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanocages acted as seeds for the growth of $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -MOF-5 and $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -MOF-5 nucleated from the surface of the ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ nanocages to one specific direction outward and grew to form a nanotube-like structure with increasing reaction time. The amount of the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ seeds had a major influence in determining the formation of the starfish-like structure as the starfish-shaped morphology was not formed when the amount of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ seeds was too small. On the contrary, the excess addition of the ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ seeds caused the starfish-shaped structure to become vague. Thus, it was suggested that higher $\mathrm{(Zn+Fe)/Co}$ ratios could lead to the formation of more nanotubes from one nanocage. When tested for supercapacitors, the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\$ ${\mathrm{ZnFe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ electrode exhibited a high specific capacitance of $326.7\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $\begin{array}{r}{1\mathrm{A}\mathfrak{g}^{-1};}\end{array}$ , which is around three times higher than that of pure ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ $(142\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}})$ or pure ${\mathrm{ZnFe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ $(11\bar{2}\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}})$ . Furthermore, when tested at larger current densities of 2.5, 5, 7.5, and 10 A $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{g}^{-1}.}\end{array}$ , the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/Z n F e_{2}O_{4}}$ electrode showed specific capacitances of 254.5, 211.4, 189, and $169\mathrm{~F~}\mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ , respectively. The relatively low capacitance retention of the composite electrode $(51.8\%)$ was due to their relatively slow Faradaic reactions.
Despite this slight setback, the energy density of the ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}/$ ${\mathrm{ZnFe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ electrode could reach as high as $82.5~\mathrm{W~h~kg^{-1}}$ at a power density of $675~\mathrm{W~kg^{-1}}$ , which is a major improvement from the pure $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ $\mathrm{(17.8~Wh~kg^{-1}}$ at a power density of 250 $\mathbf{W_{\lambda}}\mathbf{kg}^{-1},$ ) and ${\mathrm{ZnFe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ (40.4 W $\bar{\mathrm{~h~}}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ at a power density of $722.5\mathrm{W}\mathrm{kg}^{-1},$ ) electrodes. Moreover, the energy density of the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/Z n F e_{2}O_{4}}$ electrode could be retained at $44.3\mathrm{~W~h~kg^{-1}}$ at a power density as high as $6750~\mathrm{W~kg^{-1}}$ . The improvement in the electrochemical performance was mainly attributed to the enlarged potential window as a result of the hybridization of the ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ nanocages with ${\mathrm{ZnFe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ nanotubes.
While NiO has been considered as a promising electrode material for supercapacitor applications due to its high theoretical capacitance $(>2500^{\mathrm{~\circ~}}\mathrm{~F~}\mathrm{~g~}^{-1})$ , the real specific capacitance of NiO materials is still far below the theoretical value as a result of its intrinsically poor electrical conductivity. 99 Zinc oxide $(\mathrm{ZnO})$ has been considered as one of the most attractive materials for improving the electrochemical performance of NiO in supercapacitors due to its good electrical conductivity (which is useful for providing an electrically conducting pathway), as well as its high chemical stability and mechanical flexibility. For instance, $\mathrm{NiO/ZnO}$ hollow spheres d e r i v e d f r o m y o $\displaystyle{1\mathrm{k}-s\mathrm{h}}$ e l l $\ensuremath{\mathrm{~N~i~}}-Z\ensuremath{\mathrm{~n~}}$ M O F $\left(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{C}_{8}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{O}_{4})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}\right]\cdot2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ microspheres have been shown as promising electrode materials for supercapacitors (Figure 3a).79 To prepare the $\mathtt{N i-Z n}$ MOF precursor, the metal salts (nickel and zinc nitrates) were reacted with $p$ -benzenedicarboxylic acid $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\right)$ in a mixture of DMF and ethylene glycol at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to form solid spheres. The nanorod-composed shells then self-assembled on the surface of the solid spheres during the solvothermal process to generate the yolk−shell $\mathrm{Ni/Zn{\cdot}M O F}$ microspheres (Figure ${3\mathrm{b},\mathrm{c}},$ . The conversion of the yolk−shell $\mathrm{{Ni}/\mathrm{{Zn}}}$ -MOF microspheres to double-shelled $\mathrm{NiO}/\mathrm{ZnO}$ hollow spheres (Figure 3d) was achieved through a simple calcination in air at $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . As shown in Figure 3e, the CV curves of the $\mathrm{NiO}/\mathrm{ZnO}$ electrode within a potential window of $0.0{-}0.55\mathrm{~V~}$ at various scan rates from 5 to $100\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ revealed the existence of a pair of strong redox peaks, suggesting that the capacitance characteristics mainly result from pseudocapacitive capacitance. This is further supported by the galvanostatic charge−discharge curves of the $\mathrm{NiO}/\mathrm{ZnO}$ electrode in the potential range of $0.0-$ $0.5\mathrm{~V~}$ at different current densities (Figure 3f), which showed well-defined voltage plateaus, indicating the pseudocapacitive behavior of the $\mathrm{NiO}/\mathrm{ZnO}$ composite material. The specific capacitances of the $\mathrm{NiO/ZnO}$ electrode were calculated to be 497, 436, 378, 350, and $298\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ at current densities of 1.3, 2.6, 5.2, 8, and 13.3 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively (Figure $3\mathrm{g},$ . More importantly, when the $\mathrm{NiO}/\mathrm{ZnO}$ composite electrode was evaluated for its cycling stability, it showed an increase of ${\sim}17\%$ in capacitance from 408 to $47\dot{8}\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ after 2000 cycles (Figure 3h). This increase in capacitance was due to initial activation of the electrode material, which is commonly observed in many pseudocapacitive electrode materials.
Recently, Liu et al. has developed a facile method for obtaining $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/Z n O}$ heterostructure through a two-step calcination of the core−shell structured ZIF-8@ZIF-67, first in $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ at $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ then in air at $350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ 98 The core−shell ZIF- $8\textcircled{a}$ ZIF-67 precursor which consisted of ZIF-8 and ZIF-67 crystals as the core and shell materials, respectively, was obtained via a seed-mediated growth method. In the proposed method, the ZIF-8 seeds were initially mixed with both methanolic solutions of cobalt(II) chloride and 2-methylimidazole before being reacted at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ to promote the growth of the ZIF-67 shell on the surface of the ZIF-8 crystals. When an ASC was assembled using the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/Z n O}$ heterostructure as the positive electrode and active carbon as the negative electrode in a 6 M KOH electrolyte, it delivered a very high energy density of $43.2~\mathrm{W~h~kg}^{-1}$ at a power density of 1401 W $\mathrm{kg}^{-\mathrm{i}}$ . Additionally, a high energy density of 25 W h $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ could be retained at a high power density of 13 846 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ . Furthermore, the cycling stability tests showed that the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/Z n O}$ hybrid electrode was stable up to 1000 cycles with a high capacitance retention of $93.2\%$ . The above examples demonstrate that MOFs could be effectively employed as precursors to fabricate porous metal oxide hybrids with enhanced electrochemical properties for supercapacitors.
MOF-Derived Metal Oxide/Carbon Composites. In spite of their high specific capacitances, TMOs still suffer from some major drawbacks when employed as electrode materials for supercapacitors as a result of their poor electrical conductivity, low electrode surface area, and short cycle life.13,99 Furthermore, the electrical conductivity of TMO-based electrodes decreases significantly when charged at higher charge− discharge rates due to the poor bulk electron conduction, leading to an overall increase in resistance.13 Consequently, this results in a localized contribution from the exposed electrode material close to the current collector rather than the contribution from the bulk material. Additionally, as pure TMOs are crystalline materials, the electrolyte can only diffuse for a limited distance $(\sim20\ \mathrm{nm})$ inside their pores, unlike amorphous materials where the electrolyte can diffuse for a larger distance $\sim50~\mathrm{\nm})$ .13,99 An effective strategy to overcome this problem is to combine the TMO material with a carbon-based material or to deposit the TMO material on a conductive carbon matrix. Carbon-supported metal oxide materials can achieve better stability compared to pure metal oxides in supercapacitor applications as the carbon matrix can effectively reduce mechanical stress by the synergic effects of pseudocapacitive and EDLC materials. Furthermore, the hybridization of metal oxides with carbon-based materials has been shown to be beneficial for enhancing their energy density for ASC by extending the potential window range.96,100
Recently, MOFs have been demonstrated as suitable precursors for the derivation of porous metal oxide−carbon composites due to the presence of metal nodes which can be oxidized to form metal oxides along with the existence of organic linkers which can be decomposed to form porous carbons.23 For instance, a composite material composed of graphitic carbon and chromium oxide $\left(\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\right)$ nanoribbons with a large BET surface area of $438~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ has been obtained from the carbonization of a mixture of polyfurfuryl alcohol and MIL-101 under Ar flow at $900~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .101 In this reaction system, MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ acted as both carbon and $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ sources as well as a template to accommodate furfuryl alcohol as the primary carbon source. During the calcination process, the polymerization of furfuryl alcohol occurred within the 3-D channels of $\mathrm{{MIL-}101\mathrm{{(Cr)}}}$ at around $150\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ whereas a further increase in temperature above $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ triggered the decomposition of $\mathrm{MIL-}101\mathrm{(Cr)}$ and the conversion of polyfurfuryl alcohol to graphitic carbon.
Galvanostatic charge/discharge tests of the $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/$ graphitic carbon composite electrode in the potential window of $-0.5$ to 0.1 V revealed that the composite electrode exhibited a maximum specific capacitance of $\bar{2}91\ \mathrm{F}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $0.25\mathrm{~A~g^{\bar{-}1}}$ . Furthermore, it showed a good electrochemical stability with a high capacitance retention of $95.5\%$ after 3000 cycles at $\mathrm{~1~A~g^{-1}~}$ . In such a composite electrode, the carbon component stored electrical energy from double-layer capacitance generated by charge separation at the interface between the electrode and liquid electrolyte. In comparison, the $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ nanoribbons acted as redox pseudocapacitor which stored electrical energy via fast and electrochemically reversible Faradaic redox reaction, while also contributing to the total capacitance through the double-layer effect. The outstanding capacitive performance of the $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C}$ composite electrode was attributed to several reasons including: (i) the enhanced electronic conductivity due to the presence of graphitic carbon, which facilitated the rapid transportation of electrons and ions, and (ii) the proper combination of micropores and mesopores together with polar functionalities within the carbon matrix which ensured maximum ionic wettability and storage during electrochemical tests.
Apart from binary metal oxide/carbon composites, MOFs have also been employed as precursors to derive ternary metal/ metal oxide/carbon composites. This is possible because the metal nodes in MOFs can be transformed in situ into metal nanoparticles during the carbonization process, whereas the organic linkers are converted to porous carbon materials.23,102 For example, ternary $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu}@\mathrm{C}$ composites have been synthesized from the thermolysis of $\mathrm{\small{[Cu_{2}(\bar{B}T C)_{4/3}(H_{2}O)_{2}]_{6}}}.$ - $\mathrm{\small{[POM](C_{4}H_{12}N)_{2}{\cdot}{\cdot}\mathrm{{xH}_{2}O}}}$ $\left(\mathrm{POM}\ =\ \mathrm{\left[PMo_{12}O_{40}\right]^{3-}}\right)$ $\mathbf{\widetilde{POM@}}$ MOF) crystals.102 The $\mathrm{POM}\ @\mathrm{MOF}$ crystals with polyhedral block-like morphology were obtained through the hydrothermal treatment of a mixture solution consisting of Keggintype phosphomolybdic acid $\left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PMo}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}{\cdot}n\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)$ , copper nitrate, $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}_{\mathrm{\ell}}$ , and tetramethylammonium hydroxide $((\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{4}\mathrm{NOH})$ at $180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\ \mathrm{~h~}$ Following the heat treatment under $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere at $600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the $\mathrm{POM}@\mathrm{MOF}$ crystals were successfully converted to $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu}@\mathrm{C},$ which exhibited a large BET surface area of $183.3~\mathrm{~m~}^{2}\mathrm{~g~}^{-1}$ and an average pore size of $6\ \mathrm{nm}$ . During calcination, the $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ and Cu derived from Mo-POM and Cu-MOF in $\mathrm{POM}@\mathrm{MOF}$ crystals were embedded into the porous carbon matrix. In terms of electrochemical performance, the $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu}@\mathrm{C}$ hybrid electrode showed a high specific capacitance of 28.56 mA h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $\bar{0}.5\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ . Furthermore, the symmetric supercapacitor device fabricated using these composites showed an excellent cycling stability up to 5000 charge−discharge cycles with $91\%$ capacitance retention. The good electrochemical performance of the $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu}@\mathrm{C}$ electrode was attributed to two main factors: (i) the abundant presence of micro- and mesopores, which provided pathways for transporting electrons and afforded spaces for storing $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ , thereby increasing the electrolyte diffusion and supplies for ions consumed in Faradaic reactions, respectively, and (ii) the good dispersion of the $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ phase into the highly conductive phases of Cu and carbon, which reduced the intrinsic resistance of the composite. Moreover, the CV tests revealed that the charge transfer mechanism of the $\mathrm{MoO}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu}@\mathrm{C}$ electrode is similar to that of previously reported ${\mathrm{Mo}}^{4+}$ material tested in alkaline electrolyte and can be expressed as
$$
\mathrm{MoO}_{2}+x\mathrm{K}^{+}+x e^{-}\Longleftrightarrow\mathrm{MoO}_{2-x}(\mathrm{OK})_{x}
$$
$$
\mathrm{MoO}_{2}+4\mathrm{OH}^{-}\Longleftrightarrow\mathrm{MoO}_{4}^{2-}+2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}+2e^{-}
$$
From the above examples, it is evident that the presence of a carbon matrix with a large surface area can lead to an improved dispersion of the metal oxide nanoparticles, which in turn promotes a better synergistic effect between the two materials. This synergistic effect is largely responsible for enhancing the electrochemical performance of MOF-derived metal oxide/ carbon composites for supercapacitor applications.
Apart from directly deriving the porous metal oxide/carbon composites from $\mathrm{MOFs},$ an alternative way to enhance the electrochemical performance of MOF-derived oxides for supercapacitors is to deposit them on external conductive carbon matrices (e.g., graphene oxide (rGO), graphene network, carbon nanotubes) through in situ growth processes. For example, a $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/3\mathrm{-D}$ graphene network (3DGN) hybrid derived from Mn-BTC/3DGN has shown high electrochemical performance for free-standing supercapacitors.103 To prepare this hybrid material, the 3DGN was first immersed in the MnBTC growth solution (contained $\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2},$ polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP), and $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC})$ ) and aged for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ at room temperature to promote the in situ growth of Mn-BTC crystals on the 3DGN (Figure 4a). Then, the Mn-BTC/3DGN hybrid was calcined in air at $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to convert it to $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/3\mathrm{DGN}$ . The deposited $\mathrm{{Mn}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}_{3}$ particles exhibited a nanowire stacking flower-like morphology with a diameter of several micrometers and a rough surface contributed by the release of gaseous molecules during calcination. In terms of electrochemical performance, the $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/3\mathrm{DGN}$ electrode exhibited high specific capacitances of 471.1, 403.1, 374.2, 326.5, and 270.0 $\hat{\textbf{F}}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at current densities of 0.2, 0.5, 1, 2, and 5 A $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{g}^{-1},}\end{array}$ respectively (Figure 4b). The decrease in specific capacitance with increasing discharge current densities was attributed to the increased resistance in $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ and the relatively insufficient Faradaic redox reaction at higher current densities. The excellent rate capability of the $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/3\mathrm{DGN}$ electrode was contributed by the hierarchically interconnected and porous structure of the materials, which could enable the facile penetration of electrolytes to promote the redox reactions and diffusion of ions during the rapid charge−discharge process. Furthermore, as depicted in Figure 4c, the specific capacitance of the $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/3\mathrm{DGN}$ electrode remained unchanged even after 1800 charge−discharge cycles, indicating its excellent cycling stability. The good electrochemical stability of this composite electrode was attributed to the macroporous 3DGN which provided an easy access for the electrolyte and prevented the active materials from falling off into the electrolyte, as well as serving as a structural buffer for the large volume expansioncontraction during the redox reaction.
In another example, rGO has been used as a carbon support for the deposition of ZIF-derived $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ to form $\mathrm{rGO}@\mathbb{C}_{9_{3}}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and sandwich-structured $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}r G O\mathrm{-}C o_{3}O_{4}}$ hybrids.104 To obtain the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}\mathrm{-rGO-Co_{3}O_{4}}}$ hybrid, sandwich-structured ZIF-67-GO-ZIF-67 hybrids were first synthesized by dispersing the GO solution in a methanol solution containing PVP, cobalt(II) nitrate, as well as 2-methylimidazole and reacting them for 6 h. Next, the obtained ZIF-67-GO-ZIF-67 hybrid was first calcined in Ar atmosphere at $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ and then in air at the same temperature to achieve the porous $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{-r}\mathrm{GO}\cdot$ - $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ hybrid. In comparison, the $\mathrm{rGO}@\mathbb{C}_{0_{3}}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ particles were obtained by heating the ${\bf G}{\bf O}(\varpi\mathrm{ZIF}{-}67\$ particles under the same conditions as ZIF-67-GO-ZIF-67. The formation of the sandwich structure in the case of ZIF-67-GO-ZIF-67 was attributed to the addition of water as a solvent, which promoted faster depletion of ions and molecules that fed the particles at the particle/solution interface in different solvents. In the case of the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}\mathrm{-rGO\mathrm{-}C o_{3}O_{4}}}$ hybrid, ZIF-67 particles with diameters ranging from 700 to $1000\ \mathrm{nm}$ were homogeneously deposited on both sides of GO sheets to form a sandwich-like structure, as a result of the electrostatic attraction between the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ particles and $\mathrm{rGO}$ . The BET specific surface areas of the ZIF-67-derived $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4},$ $\Gamma\mathrm{GO}@{\mathrm{Co}_{3}}{\mathrm{O}_{4}},$ and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}\mathrm{-rGO-Co_{3}O_{4}}}$ were measured to be 32.54, 198.54, and $494.54~\mathrm{~m}^{2}~\mathrm{~g}^{-1}.$ , respectively, indicating the increase in specific surface area with the addition of $\mathbf{rGO}$ .
When tested for supercapacitors, the $\mathrm{rGO}@\mathbb{C}\circ_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ electrode displayed specific capacitances of 546, 536, 526, and $496\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ at current densities of 0.5, 1, 2, and 5 A $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{g}^{-1}.}\end{array}$ , respectively. Furthermore, the $\mathrm{rGO}@\mathbb{C}\circ_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ electrode was highly stable, showing a high capacitance retention of $90\%$ after 10 000 cycles at $5\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ . The outstanding electrochemical performance of the $\mathrm{rGO}@\mathbb{C}_{9}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ hybrid for supercapacitors was attributed to (i) the porous nature of the hybrid which could improve the penetration of electrolyte and shorten the diffusion distance of electron/ion, but also mediated the volume change during charge−discharge cycles; (ii) the large specific surface area which could increase the contact area between the electrolyte and the electrode material; and (iii) the presence of an rGO layer which could effectively enhance the specific surface area and improved the electrical conductivity of the hybrid material by enhancing the electron transfer rate. The electrochemical properties of previously reported MOF-derived metal oxide nanostructures and metal oxide/carbon composites for supercapacitor applications are tabulated in Table 1.
Based on the above studies, it is evident that MOFs are highly attractive precursors or templates for obtaining porous metal oxide/carbon composites, due to their large surface area, controllable porosity, and tunable composition and morphology. However, to date, there are only a few reports on the use of MOF-derived oxide/carbon composites for supercapacitor applications. This is largely due to some problems associated with the derivation of porous metal oxide/carbon composites for supercapacitor applications, including the following.13
(i) When carbonized at lower temperatures, the carbon component in the metal oxide/carbon composites is poorly graphitized and highly resistive, leading to a poor electrochemical performance.   
(ii) At higher carbonization temperatures, the carbon component is highly conductive; however, the metal oxide component may be reduced to metal nanoparticles which have poor capacitance.
Electrolytes for MOF-Derived Metal Oxides. Depending on the pore size and specific surface area of the MOF-derived metal oxides, the selection of a proper electrolyte is critical for achieving a high capacitive performance. Figure 5 demonstrates the variations of different electrolyte ion size with hydrated ion size for aqueous electrolytes. Furthermore, the electrical conductivity values of these ions are shown with the color mapping of these points. Hence, if the electrolyte ion size matches the pore size of the metal oxide, it can lead to a significant improvement in the capacitive performance.
The aqueous electrolytes for metal oxides are inexpensive, highly conductive, and easy to handle, thus making them highly favorable as electrolytes at a laboratory scale. Most metal oxides are highly active in aqueous electrolytes due to their Faradaic oxidation and reduction reactions with aqueous electrolytes for charge storage.108 These Faradaic reactions can lead to a high capacitive performance $(\sim1000\mathrm{~F~g~}^{-1})$ within a small or limited potential window compared to carbon materials in nonaqueous electrolytes using organic/ionic electrolytes. The limited potential window is the major limitation for metal oxides, as gas evolution occurs if the potential window exceeds $1.2~\mathrm{V},$ which leads to both decreased performance and safety concerns. The most commonly used electrolytes for metal oxides are ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}S{\mathrm{O}}_{4},$ KOH, NaOH, ${\mathrm{Na}}_{2}S{\mathrm{O}}_{4},$ KCl, etc. We expect that by optimizing the heating conditions of MOF-derived metal oxides as well by using different MOF precursors, it will become possible to match the pore-size of MOF-derived metal oxides with the targeted ion size of the electrolytes. In this way, we can design high-performance MOF-derived metal oxides for next-generation energy storage applications.
## CONCLUSIONS
In recent years, MOFs have emerged as highly attractive templates or precursors for the derivations of porous transition metal oxide nanomaterials due to their highly designable structures, tunable composition, large surface area, and controllable porosity. By carefully controlling the calcination conditions (e.g., atmosphere, calcination temperature, and time), MOFs which are made up of metal ion centers coordinated to organic linkers can be converted into porous metal oxide nanostructures or nanocomposites.
Present Obstacles and Possible Solutions. MOFderived metal oxide nanostructures have shown some good potential as electrode materials for supercapacitor applications; however, they still suffer from some major drawbacks. Here, we will provide our perspectives on some key problems in this field. These problems are
(i) poor electrical conductivity at higher charge−discharge rates due to poor bulk electron conduction, leading to an overall increase in resistance   
(ii) low electrode surface area, resulting in a small contact area between the electrode material and the electrolyte   
(iii) short cycle life at higher rates due to the increase in resistance and insufficient Faradaic redox reactions at higher current densities   
(iv) limitations in the diffusion distance of the electrolyte inside the pores of the metal oxides due to the high crystallinity of MOF-derived metal oxides.
In order to improve the electrochemical performance of MOF-derived metal oxides for supercapacitor applications, we propose the following strategies to address each of these issues including:
(i,iii) The electrical conductivity can be improved by combining the MOF-derived metal oxides with highly conductive carbon substrates (e.g., rGO, carbon nanotubes, and graphene networks) together with a secondary metal oxide component.
(ii) The large increase in surface area of MOF-derived metal oxides can be achieved by preheating the MOF precursors under $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere before heat treating them in air. This is beneficial for preventing the rapid release of volatile gases (e.g., $\mathrm{CO}_{x}$ and $\mathrm{NO}_{x})$ which would have caused the collapse of the frameworks.
(iv) The improvement in the distance at which the electrolyte can diffuse inside the pores of the metal oxides can be obtained by reducing the crystallinity of the MOFderived metal oxide materials. Previously, it has been shown that electrolyte could only diffuse for a limited distance ${\binom{\prime}{\sim20}}$ nm) inside the pores of crystalline oxide materials, whereas in amorphous oxide materials, the same electrolyte could diffuse for a larger distance $(\sim50\mathrm{nm})$ . Furthermore, this problem may also be solved by matching the pore size of the MOF-derived materials with the electrolyte ion size through optimization of the heating conditions.
Versatile Design of MOF-Derived Metal Oxides. There is a huge scope for the development of phase-controlled, highly conductive MOF-derived metal oxides for supercapacitor applications. We expect that in the near future, the functionalization of MOF-derived metal oxides through heteroatomic doping or hybridization with carbonaceous materials will help to realize the potential use of MOF-derived metal oxides for high-performance supercapacitors. More interestingly, the parent MOF can be designed in 1-D, 2-D, and 3-D depending on the organic and inorganic constituents involved in the synthesis process. Thus, by optimizing the heating conditions, nanoporous metal oxide materials which retained the original shape and the high surface area of the parent MOFs can be obtained. Such a flexible control is very important considering the different requirements needed for different applications. For instance, some applications, such as frequency regulators, require materials with high power densities. The 1-D and 2-D materials can provide easy access for ion intercalation and deintercalation, thus providing rapid charge−discharge and ultrahigh reversibility (high power density, Scheme 3). In comparison, high energy density applications, such as mass rapid transit, need a higher charge storage capacity. This may be achieved through the use of 3-D materials which can provide many reaction sites in their 3-D networks. However, very few conventional methods offer the options for controlling the dimensionalities of the obtained metal oxide products for the desired applications. This clearly highlights the advantage of MOF-derived materials and their future significance.
Furthermore, with respect to the commercialization of MOFderived metal oxides, it is highly important that they are tested thoroughly through robust charge−discharge tests (at least ${>}200000$ cycles) to match the practical standard of commercial carbon electrodes. Hence, in the future, significant research efforts should be carried out for the synthetic method modifications of MOF-derived metal oxides and to increase their processability for commercial applications. Finally, a
